# The capture writes six frames by default; nineteen of the frames in this
# folder are committed as documentation -- four of the recorded match, one of
# the effects fixture, three of the four melee and impact lines standing on the
# recorded board, three of the two pierce lines standing on it, four of the
# three magic lines standing on it and four of the six creep rows that carry an
# aura or a pool walking it. README.md says which nineteen and why.
#
# Everything else it writes must not be committed, and that cannot be left to
# whoever runs the capture next. The fourth working agreement is explicit about
# it: "if a generated file must **not** be committed, that has to be arranged by
# construction — an ignore rule — and not by remembering."
#
# Without this file, a plain `tools/capture-match-frames.ps1` leaves untracked
# PNGs sitting in the tree, and the build gate's last step — the one
# that fails a push whose run rewrote the repository — goes red for a reason
# that has nothing to do with the push.
#
# The negations are not load-bearing today, because an ignore rule cannot
# govern a file git already tracks. They are here so that re-adding any of these
# frames after a deliberate deletion works without anybody having to remember
# this file exists.
*.png
!match-tick-1096.png
!match-tick-1229.png
!match-tick-1546.png
!match-tick-2700.png
!effects-roster-tick-0700.png
!four-lines-tick-0572.png
!four-lines-tick-0780.png
!four-lines-tick-0813.png
!pierce-lines-tick-0516.png
!pierce-lines-tick-0673.png
!pierce-lines-tick-0674.png
!magic-lines-tick-0311.png
!magic-lines-tick-0331.png
!magic-lines-tick-0342.png
!magic-lines-tick-0344.png
!creep-auras-tick-0094.png
!creep-auras-tick-0271.png
!creep-auras-tick-0272.png
!creep-auras-tick-0276.png

# The one sheet out of the armed-roster capture that is kept: the four props
# that stand on the ground beside a tower, at the sizes they are drawn at.
# Named rather than covered by a pattern, and not the tool's own
# candidates-sheet.png -- that name is written again by every candidate run,
# so a committed file under it would be rewritten by somebody asking an
# unrelated question. This is a copy under a name nothing else writes.
!roster/beside-props-sheet.png

# And the one out of the melee art pass: the nine rungs of the Knight, the
# Barbarian and the Paladin drawn as their rows are now bound. Named for the
# same reason as the sheet above -- the tool writes candidates-sheet.png on
# every set run, so the committed copy carries a name nothing else writes.
!roster/melee-lines-sheet.png

# And the one out of the caster art pass: the nine rungs of the Cleric, the
# Mage and the Druid, kept under a name of its own for the same reason.
!roster/caster-lines-sheet.png

# And the one out of the pierce-and-turret art pass: the nine rungs of the
# Archer, the Rogue and the Engineer, kept under a name of its own for the same
# reason.
!roster/pierce-turret-lines-sheet.png

# And the one out of the first creep art pass: the six bodies of ids 39 to 46,
# with the Shade's four atlases and the Frost Wight's two axes beside them as
# the two questions that sheet asks. Same naming rule again.
!roster/creep-bodies-sheet.png

# And the one out of the second creep art pass, which empties the stand-in
# list: the six bodies of ids 38, 43, 44 and 47 to 49, with the Grave Robber's
# backpack held rather than worn and the Witch's broom turned upright beside
# them as the two questions that sheet asks. Same naming rule again.
!roster/creep-bodies-rest-sheet.png

# And three out of the roster sign-off's first prototype ticket, each the one
# sheet its own question is asked on. Same naming rule as every sheet above --
# the tool writes candidates-sheet.png on every set run, so a committed copy
# carries a name nothing else writes.
#
#   the Paladin line's three rungs in thirty-two clips, because ids 20, 21 and
#   22 ship posed by nothing and stand in their import bind pose;
#   the Witch's broom and the Necromancer's scythe at five turns each, because
#   both lie flat in the fist;
#   the Grave Robber with the sheathed sword and without, which needed the
#   set file's !Node suffix because the blade is in the body mesh.
!roster/paladin-clips-sheet.png
!roster/prop-turns-sheet.png
!roster/grave-robber-sword-sheet.png

# And three frames of the recorded match at tick 1546, the same tick and the
# same distance as the committed match-tick-1546.png, at three camera pitches.
# NOT a sheet -- the armed-roster tool stitches contact sheets and the match
# capture does not, and the thing being compared is a whole 1600x900 frame
# rather than a body on a plain ground. Three and not the seven that were
# rendered: the shipped pitch, the shallowest that still frames the board and
# one steeper, which is the bracket. The other four regenerate from the one
# command in roster/mage-hat-pitch.txt.
!roster/mage-hat-pitch-20.png
!roster/mage-hat-pitch-35-shipped.png
!roster/mage-hat-pitch-55.png

# And the ten screen captures of the built player, which no capture tool writes
# and nothing here regenerates: a whole ten-wave run on the wide roster, driven
# by synthetic input. Named one at a time like every exception above, so the
# next screenshot somebody drops into this folder is ignored until somebody
# decides to keep it. played-run/README.md says what each one is.
!played-run/build-phase.png
!played-run/the-nine-lines-close.png
!played-run/capstone-consecration.png
!played-run/capstone-mortar.png
!played-run/capstone-fan-of-knives.png
!played-run/resolution.png
!played-run/black-knight-beside-towers.png
!played-run/a-slow-nobody-can-see.png
!played-run/the-same-slow-close.png
!played-run/run-over.png

# The armed-roster capture's set run writes a manifest beside its PNGs, saying
# which tile of the contact sheet is which. Same rule as the frames: it is
# regenerated by whoever wants to look, it is nobody's documentation to keep,
# and an untracked file here fails the gate's tree-clean step for a reason that
# has nothing to do with the push. README.md is the only tracked prose in this
# folder and is not a .txt. Named exactly rather than as *.txt, so a file
# somebody does mean to keep here is not swallowed by a rule written for this
# one.
candidates-manifest.txt
